    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen trying to do some custom handling of `vim.event.lspprogress`, I encountered the following issue: tables with numbers as keys behave differently depending on the keys' values.\n\nHere's a repro.lua file:\n\n```lua\nlocal oneliner = function(...) return vim.inspect(..., { depth = 1, newline = \"\" }) end\nlocal printer = function(f) vim.print(oneliner(f)) end\nlocal printer_kv = function(k, v) vim.print((\"[%s]: %s\"):format(k, oneliner(v))) end\n\nlocal good = { [435] = { this = \"is fine\", } }\nlocal bad = { [0] = { this = \"is not fine\", } }\n\n-- Should print \"{ this = \"is fine\", }\"\n-- And it does!\nvim.iter(good):each(printer)\n\nvim.print(\"======\")\n\n-- Should print \"{ this = \"is not fine\", }\"\n-- Actually prints \"0\"\nvim.iter(bad):each(printer)\n-- Same behaviour here, table (the value) gets reduced to \"0\"\n-- Prints [1] = 0 when it should print \"[0] = { this = \"is not fine\" }\"\nvim.iter(bad):enumerate():each(printer_kv)\n```\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nnvim --clean repro.lua\n:luafile repro.lua\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe last two statements should print something akin to `{this = \"is not fine\"}`.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nLatest nightly, and nvim 0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nFedora 44\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nwezterm\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nfedora repos (official) + nightly from your releases",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Looking at [the init code when `vim.iter(...)` is called](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/8e0e19c08bdb6ca7cf6f4062d55510b0272592cc/runtime/lua/vim/iter.lua#L1271-L1278), it does look like it's forced into being a list if the first index is 0 or below.\n\nIs there not a way I could tell it \"yes, this is a table and treat it as such\" ? Maybe a field in the table's `metatable`?",
