            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Yes, this is correct, and simply how Lua works: Everything is a table, but arrays (tables with integer keys) and lists (arrays starting from 1 and having no gaps) have additional contracts that are exploited in `vim.iter` for performance reasons.\n\nThe reason I stumbled into this issue was because while processing `LspProgress` events from `emmylua_ls`, the LSP server sends the first progress event with token/ID 0. It doesn't seem to happen with a couple other LSP servers I use. Since I'm essentially at the whims of the LSP server, I wondered if there was a way to force-consider it a table.\n\n> Hmm `vim.islist(good)` & `vim.islist(bad)` are false, so according to those rules both lists should behave the same 🤔\n\nEither that or `vim.islist` has a problem.\n\nFor the moment, I solved the issue I was personally having by indexing the `LspProgress` events not by the \"raw\" token, but by this:\n\n```lua\nlocal function tokenize(token) return \"token-\" .. tostring(token) end\nlocal token = tokenize(data.params.token)\n-- Continue processing as normal\n```",
