        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Btw, Christian: you say arrays are tables with integer keys, and are used differently that dict-like tables in `vim.iter`. But this doesn't make sense with the example of the `good` table in the original repro script: it only has one integer key (`435`) but gets treated as a \"regular\" table as far as I can tell.\n\nTake the following variation of the original script:\n\n```lua\nlocal oneliner = function(...) return vim.inspect(..., { depth = 1, newline = \"\" }) end\nlocal printer = function(f) vim.print(oneliner(f)) end\nlocal printer_kv = function(k, v) vim.print((\"[%s]: %s\"):format(k, oneliner(v))) end\n\nlocal good = { [435] = { this = \"is fine\", } }\n\n-- Should print \"{ this = \"is fine\", }\"\n-- And it does!\nvim.iter(good):each(printer)\n\nvim.iter(good):enumerate():each(printer_kv) -- (1)\n```\n\nShouldn't the line `(1)` print `\"[435] = { this = \"is fine\", }\"` ? Instead, it prints `\"[1] = { this = \"is fine\", }\"`. If this is not what's supposed to happen, this might be better off as a separate issue.",
